  and sudden reverse of expectations when reading
  the rest of the sentence, hidden until then at the
  top of the article.

  Fun, fun, fun.

This situation has been a running sore debate here
in rgrn for several years. The "underlying
complaint" here has always been that some here are
using badly designed news reading clients that fail
to display subjects contiguous to article contents,
and so leave their users adrift.

For many, many articles, the short article body does
not provide enough context to guess the subject
line, leaving the authors' intent a mystery to the
users of news reader client malware that lets the
subject line go missing from proximity to the
article body.

The "control freakery" problem is that when the
users of broken news reader clients are
short-changed of access to the subject lines by
their software, and become confused about the
article meaning, they chose, inappropriately, to
complain, and here in public, to the article
authors, rather than choose, appropriately, to
complain, and in private, to the news reader client
software vendors of that broken software.

Things go swiftly south from there, as the maligned
article authors take exception to being pilloried
for a problem which is not of their making and is
not one they should be tasked to solve

  [by changing a behavior

    [using subject lines that are unrepeated
    introductory parts of the article and crucial to
    understanding the sense of the article],

  that works for all rgrn participants, and for all
  Usenet participants in general, _except_ those who
  choose to use broken news reader client software].

Misplacing blame, and doing so in an annoying and
dictatorial fashion, is of course a recipe for
instant warfare, just open news article.

That recipe gets used here much too often, by people
who should have better sense and manners.

